            <description><![CDATA[<style>
body { font-family: -apple-system, 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif; font-size: 13px; color: #1a1a1a; margin: 12px; }
h2 { font-size: 15px; margin: 0 0 8px; } ul { margin: 4px 0 10px; padding-left: 20px; } li { margin-bottom: 3px; }
p { margin: 4px 0 10px; }
</style>
<h2>Asobi 0.2 (build 187)</h2>
<p><b>Client / Reviewer mode.</b> Invite someone into a review by name, at the access level you choose, and send them a single file to open.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Session ▸ Invite a Reviewer…</b> — name them, pick their tier, save the invite. Opening it puts them in the review: session, passcode and identity all carried, nothing to configure. Their name comes from the invite, so their first comment is attributed correctly.</li>
<li><b>Reviewer</b> — playback, compare, annotation, comments, marking a preferred version, note and report export, and the looks you saved to the session bank.</li>
<li><b>Expert reviewer</b> — all of that plus the review grade, printer lights, scopes, their own LUTs and Film Lab. For directors who like to tinker.</li>
<li>Neither tier reaches the pipeline: no conform, no relink, no packaging.</li>
<li>A guest sees their tier at the bottom-left of the viewport, and <b>Session ▸ Sign Out of Review</b> returns their copy to full mode.</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Live joining still needs a shared network</b> — the same LAN or VPN. Off-network, an invite still sets up the reviewer's app correctly for the send-a-session workflow, but it can't reach a live session yet.</p>
]]></description>
